      "name": {
        "name": "Small heat shock protein",
        "short": "Small_HSP"
      },
      "description": [
        {
          "text": "<p>Prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms respond to heat shock or other environmental stress by inducing the synthesis of proteins collectively known\nas heat-shock proteins (hsp) [[cite:PUB00000102]]. Amongst them is a family of proteins with an average molecular weight of 20 Kd, known as the hsp20 proteins [[cite:PUB00003438], [cite:PUB00018014], [cite:PUB00005421]]. These seem to act as chaperones that can protect other proteins against heat-induced denaturation and aggregation. Hsp20 proteins seem to form large heterooligomeric aggregates.</p>\n\n<p>Structurally, this family is characterised by the presence of a conserved C-terminal domain of about 100 residues.</p>",
          "llm": false,
          "checked": false,
          "updated": false
        }
      ],
